, "title": "Myth, genealogy, and ritual"}, {"id": "1507e01c-80b4-4d0c-9d4f-463c48e22b28", "type": "concept", "title": "Translation and survival"}], "description": "Students encounter major cultural worlds and their narrative, poetic, and historical forms."}, {"id": "0f4ee17a-e065-4ce8-843b-38fe71667197", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Reading ethically across distance", "children": [{"id": "5ab07ac8-a02d-4a2c-95d3-7cdb9e270e32", "type": "concept", "title": "Authorship and collectivity"}, {"id": "9df53534-f2f1-444f-bbe9-1fd346c6299f", "type": "concept", "title": "Sacred and political uses of narrative"}, {"id": "064213f6-46e7-4582-bb1d-318f18122524", "type": "concept", "title": "Colonial filters"}, {"id": "4fc76a93-b08c-4898-a00e-fa1141305234", "type": "concept", "title": "Respectful interpretation"}], "description": "Students learn to avoid treating Indigenous materials as mere background for colonial writing."}]}, {"id": "e9ba2eb0-bd29-4365-89d0-11556de650d2", "type": "topic", "title": "Conquest, Colonial Chronicles, and Baroque Writing", "description": "This unit studies how conquest and colonization generated competing narratives of power, witness, conversion, resistance, and identity.", "children": [{"id": "881beffe-5ad6-4666-817c-012397be7c74", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Chronicles and conquest narratives", "children": [{"id": "5b2d038d-a507-4545-9922-740c287ad84b", "type": "concept", "title": "Eyewitness authority"}, {"id": "e5ada73e-9c54-46de-b596-8f601727a204", "type": "concept", "title": "Rhetoric of wonder"}, {"id": "449a27d1-9cb2-4cb2-b7c2-f949506640a5", "type": "concept", "title": "Imperial justification"}, {"id": "4234c062-2dd7-4262-abc2-c23d0b2bb47b", "type": "concept", "title": "Indigenous and mestizo counter-narratives"}], "description": "Students analyze chronicles as literary constructions, political documents, and contested accounts of violence."}, {"id": "81aacbe1-e05c-4e7f-94ba-2dc2a25b6db0", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Colonial subjectivity", "children": [{"id": "c888d082-4fdf-482a-97f3-e46a3d15da14", "type": "concept", "title": "Creole identity"}, {"id": "76d4e6f3-ac2e-4bd7-9928-a616fd3d821a", "type": "concept", "title": "Mestizaje"}, {"id": "02bc5545-23e0-4887-9d47-13d3b4cdcc12", "type": "concept", "title": "Conversion and evangelization"}, {"id": "b653a179-433a-453e-9852-bfca6f556e48", "type": "concept", "title": "Colonial education and literacy"}], "description": "Students examine how writers negotiated race, caste, gender, religion, and imperial hierarchy."}, {"id": "1957c5b6-c6d9-43a9-a8f6-fc1d93235c19", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Baroque and colonial poetics", "children": [{"id": "88cb8165-dade-4524-8c0e-53af1e22b7d4", "type": "concept", "title": "Sor Juana Ines de la Cruz"}, {"id": "83cb4503-388d-461c-9e7a-ecc2605eb08d", "type": "concept", "title": "Baroque complexity"}, {"id": "7465355e-8ebf-4f44-89be-5bb4224c1870", "type": "concept", "title": "Religious and secular learning"}, {"id": "46413cd7-3350-4843-8f7b-428dd683b16a", "type": "concept", "title": "Gendered authorship"}], "description": "Students connect literary style to intellectual life, gender, and power in the colonial world."}]}, {"id": "d207fbce-521c-46f9-8d4d-1dd85b8b1d25", "type": "topic", "title": "Independence, Nation Formation, and Nineteenth-Century Letters", "description": "Students study literature as a tool for imagining new nations and debating citizenship, race, education, civilization, and modernity.", "children": [{"id": "2051c234-be93-43af-8003-1d72d1464da9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Independence writing", "children": [{"id": "c8b4367a-00d9-47c0-8595-032a20e29716", "type": "concept", "title": "Revolutionary rhetoric"}, {"id": "36c76364-8fdb-4c43-a818-ee1a396f3900", "type": "concept", "title": "Republican ideals"}, {"id": "1cb822c1-b8ab-406d-9c12-77f3549df812", "type": "concept", "title": "Print culture"}, {"id": "986f752b-dfc5-4766-9807-50795e16060b", "type": "concept", "title": "Heroes, memory, and myth"}], "