s foreground Black Atlantic, Caribbean, and Afro-descendant literary traditions often underrepresented in basic surveys."}]}, {"id": "63431fd3-db79-4386-a4db-ea58380f4dc6", "type": "topic", "title": "The Latin American Boom and Magical Realism", "description": "This unit studies the international visibility of the Boom while resisting the idea that Latin American literature can be reduced to magical realism.", "children": [{"id": "ff358526-dcf6-4661-87e3-4e7843471725", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Boom narrative innovation", "children": [{"id": "7a57cc6c-1cc4-4f07-852c-9529eccdd800", "type": "concept", "title": "Nonlinear narration"}, {"id": "efa47526-fa08-489c-b1a4-64391a4a2f84", "type": "concept", "title": "Multiple perspectives"}, {"id": "3e5accef-116d-4033-abbf-38808913b03f", "type": "concept", "title": "Metafiction"}, {"id": "568baeed-978f-40c9-a8de-ca3ca542d6e0", "type": "concept", "title": "Urban and political complexity"}], "description": "Students examine formal experimentation in novels that changed global perceptions of Latin American fiction."}, {"id": "72f1f0b4-017c-44b4-ad59-6ec133aa73ae", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Magical realism and its contexts", "children": [{"id": "ce4e1700-07e4-4301-b7e8-b00e477b42c3", "type": "concept", "title": "Everyday marvelous"}, {"id": "02d2bb76-3c6c-4eae-9235-0d15373daec0", "type": "concept", "title": "Community and myth"}, {"id": "b54dda64-5ce6-458f-a9bf-c695f4076958", "type": "concept", "title": "History and violence"}, {"id": "ca5a3a71-f905-4a59-96c6-8880c70554ce", "type": "concept", "title": "Reception and commodification"}], "description": "Students distinguish magical realism from fantasy, folklore, and market stereotypes."}, {"id": "3e32ee5d-bd81-4c26-bb81-fe3ed20669c9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Canon formation", "children": [{"id": "039b83a9-8130-4822-9800-b426ea1751a7", "type": "concept", "title": "Garcia Marquez, Cortazar, Fuentes, Vargas Llosa"}, {"id": "1a7f2ccf-21ff-446a-bc8c-3ee064a61272", "type": "concept", "title": "Publishing in Barcelona and beyond"}, {"id": "75d8a000-9e08-4d05-9e7d-f58ae4e9cadc", "type": "concept", "title": "Women writers and exclusions"}, {"id": "f695f848-1e5d-4cd6-992d-2e6fb8199cff", "type": "concept", "title": "World literature circuits"}], "description": "Students ask who became visible, who was left out, and how publishing markets shaped the Boom."}]}, {"id": "369cb80c-1499-43ef-92c4-5dc38b54b0bc", "type": "topic", "title": "Dictatorship, Testimonio, Memory, and Human Rights", "description": "Students study literature's role in witnessing state violence, preserving memory, and debating truth, representation, and justice.", "children": [{"id": "13648bf0-ed98-4bb6-b7d4-1fc97e16403e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Dictatorship and repression", "children": [{"id": "5aa3bc3c-28b1-4697-b851-aac68c66cab4", "type": "concept", "title": "Southern Cone dictatorships"}, {"id": "f1a721a4-a1f4-4abf-9ae3-05ba5822b989", "type": "concept", "title": "Central American conflicts"}, {"id": "89d618ca-6e46-4504-9c4f-b271e0a47bb5", "type": "concept", "title": "Exile writing"}, {"id": "1b4657e5-f2f1-488e-8f9a-c86fd7db3649", "type": "concept", "title": "Allegory and coded critique"}], "description": "Students connect literary form to censorship, exile, fear, and authoritarian power."}, {"id": "93a1ceae-c079-4ed1-9ba9-bf0252da1528", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Testimonio and witness", "children": [{"id": "54bbf9dd-41d0-4392-8468-d7f0042c0344", "type": "concept", "title": "I, we, and community voice"}, {"id": "de73dfff-076d-4afc-a80a-7e080759ebdb", "type": "concept", "title": "Editor and narrator"}, {"id": "6d3a1ad5-cd4e-4882-b83c-4584ee8db79b", "type": "concept", "title": "Authenticity debates"}, {"id": "a04ec273-2830-4924-aaa6-6ebd665f0994", "type": "concept", "title": "Ethics of reading suffering"}], "description": "Students analyze first-person narrative, collective voice, mediation, and the politics of truth."}, {"id": "fa4c5144-0d45-452f-9b55-688414ae6