oncept", "title": "Myth, genealogy, and ritual"}, {"id": "1507e01c-80b4-4d0c-9d4f-463c48e22b28", "type": "concept", "title": "Translation and survival"}], "description": "Students encounter major cultural worlds and their narrative, poetic, and historical forms."}, {"id": "0f4ee17a-e065-4ce8-843b-38fe71667197", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Reading ethically across distance", "children": [{"id": "5ab07ac8-a02d-4a2c-95d3-7cdb9e270e32", "type": "concept", "title": "Authorship and collectivity"}, {"id": "9df53534-f2f1-444f-bbe9-1fd346c6299f", "type": "concept", "title": "Sacred and political uses of narrative"}, {"id": "064213f6-46e7-4582-bb1d-318f18122524", "type": "concept", "title": "Colonial filters"}, {"id": "4fc76a93-b08c-4898-a00e-fa1141305234", "type": "concept", "title": "Respectful interpretation"}], "description": "Students learn to avoid treating Indigenous materials as mere background for colonial writing."}]}, {"id": "e9ba2eb0-bd29-4365-89d0-11556de650d2", "type": "topic", "title": "Conquest, Colonial Chronicles, and Baroque Writing", "description": "This unit studies how conquest and colonization generated competing narratives of power, witness, conversion, resistance, and identity.", "children": [{"id": "881beffe-5ad6-4666-817c-012397be7c74", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Chronicles and conquest narratives", "children": [{"id": "5b2d038d-a507-4545-9922-740c287ad84b", "type": "concept", "title": "Eyewitness authority"}, {"id": "e5ada73e-9c54-46de-b596-8f601727a204", "type": "concept", "title": "Rhetoric of wonder"}, {"id": "449a27d1-9cb2-4cb2-b7c2-f949506640a5", "type": "concept", "title": "Imperial justification"}, {"id": "4234c062-2dd7-4262-abc2-c23d0b2bb47b", "type": "concept", "title": "Indigenous and mestizo counter-narratives"}], "description": "Students analyze chronicles as literary constructions, political documents, and contested accounts of violence."}, {"id": "81aacbe1-e05c-4e7f-94ba-2dc2a25b6db0", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Colonial subjectivity", "children": [{"id": "c888d082-4fdf-482a-97f3-e46a3d15da14", "type": "concept", "title": "Creole identity"}, {"id": "76d4e6f3-ac2e-4bd7-9928-a616fd3d821a", "type": "concept", "title": "Mestizaje"}, {"id": "02bc5545-23e0-4887-9d47-13d3b4cdcc12", "type": "concept", "title": "Conversion and evangelization"}, {"id": "b653a179-433a-453e-9852-bfca6f556e48", "type": "concept", "title": "Colonial education and literacy"}], "description": "Students examine how writers negotiated race, caste, gender, religion, and imperial hierarchy."}, {"id": "1957c5b6-c6d9-43a9-a8f6-fc1d93235c19", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Baroque and colonial poetics", "children": [{"id": "88cb8165-dade-4524-8c0e-53af1e22b7d4", "type": "concept", "title": "Sor Juana Ines de la Cruz"}, {"id": "83cb4503-388d-461c-9e7a-ecc2605eb08d", "type": "concept", "title": "Baroque complexity"}, {"id": "7465355e-8ebf-4f44-89be-5bb4224c1870", "type": "concept", "title": "Religious and secular learning"}, {"id": "46413cd7-3350-4843-8f7b-428dd683b16a", "type": "concept", "title": "Gendered authorship"}], "description": "Students connect literary style to intellectual life, gender, and power in the colonial world."}]}, {"id": "d207fbce-521c-46f9-8d4d-1dd85b8b1d25", "type": "topic", "title": "Independence, Nation Formation, and Nineteenth-Century Letters", "description": "Students study literature as a tool for imagining new nations and debating citizenship, race, education, civilization, and modernity.", "children": [{"id": "2051c234-be93-43af-8003-1d72d1464da9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Independence writing", "children": [{"id": "c8b4367a-00d9-47c0-8595-032a20e29716", "type": "concept", "title": "Revolutionary rhetoric"}, {"id": "36c76364-8fdb-4c43-a818-ee1a396f3900", "type": "concept", "title": "Republican ideals"}, {"id": "1cb822c1-b8ab-406d-9c12-77f3549df812", "type": "concept", "title": "Print culture"}, {"id": "986f752b-dfc5-4766-9807-50795e16060b", "type": "concept", "title": "Heroes, memory, and myth"}], "description": "Students connect political rupture to essays, speeches, poetry, and public intellectual culture."}, {"id": "82f6c80e-76d3-4631-85ca-1eb016e93f7c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Romanticism and costumbrismo", "children": [{"id": "a3ef2694-315d-4ef6-92bd-2a63aa536f4e", "type": "concept", "title": "Romantic subjectivity"}, {"id": "9f152a4d-f04f-4dab-ab6e-345eeb4756da", "type": "concept", "title": "Costumbrista sketches"}, {"id": "5cd8ab0c-13e5-4b31-9397-2de1a4326a57", "type": "concept", "title": "Landscape and nation"}, {"id": "4ba0fb65-b750-44a7-8e71-9ee5e91dc568", "type": "concept", "title": "Gender and domestic ideology"}], "description": "Students learn how sentiment, local color, and social types helped define national imaginaries."}, {"id": "eece777f-05f3-480d-aca9-e308c4b554e8", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Civilization and barbarism debates", "children": [{"id": "30ae1969-ee5c-4cb0-b297-1769e8da66fe", "type": "concept", "title": "Sarmiento and liberal modernity"}, {"id": "bac36fe3-6c4c-48e7-94f4-d3ccfdee603e", "type": "concept", "title": "Rural versus urban imaginaries"}, {"id": "a80874cd-ffb0-4782-a5e6-f3b654c3bfff", "type": "concept", "title": "Indigenous and Afro-descendant exclusion"}, {"id": "911af1eb-19f5-4c10-9b04-1a1892e31a00", "type": "concept", "title": "Education and citizenship"}], "description": "Students analyze how literature staged conflicts over race, region, education, and state power."}]}, {"id": "644afd69-2ba9-4b01-bfd9-1380568dab70", "type": "topic", "title": "Modernismo and Cosmopolitan Aesthetics", "description": "Modernismo reshaped Spanish-language literary style through musicality, cosmopolitanism, formal innovation, and a new sense of the writer's cultural authority.", "children": [{"id": "b446fb71-40fb-4f67-a47e-cf1ec734b846", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Aesthetic renovation", "children": [{"id": "0632a5e2-b915-4fec-a94c-7f6fd8eeef99", "type": "concept", "title": "Ruben Dario"}, {"id": "cd8fcb0a-d67e-40d2-9729-9787c1a34012", "type": "concept", "title": "Symbolism and Parnassianism"}, {"id": "2361e692-02fd-4372-9b38-e9416d4ced2d", "type": "concept", "title": "Musicality and imagery"}, {"id": "f9932f72-bc91-41bd-99ea-0da244f155bd", "type": "concept", "title": "Art for art's sake"}], "description": "Students learn why modernismo was a turning point in Spanish-language poetry and prose."}, {"id": "20c5ed84-a604-4f27-b073-135e2cd3d6fb", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Cosmopolitanism and modernity", "children": [{"id": "aa0a4390-ee5f-45da-b7ec-0b5a2b6265d1", "type": "concept", "title": "Paris and the Americas"}, {"id": "668189f4-faf6-41ae-8452-27adee198024", "type": "concept", "title": "Consumer culture"}, {"id": "197e07de-21de-43b4-9c8e-036d422e8a00", "type": "concept", "title": "New journalism"}, {"id": "137fdfb0-5e23-489f-8cc8-04ada05a2a8d", "type": "concept", "title": "Artist and marketplace"}], "description": "Students examine tensions among Europe, the Americas, urban life, markets, and cultural prestige."}, {"id": "d986fd4c-ec6c-4a9a-a2df-a415fc97edc2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Modernista legacies", "children": [{"id": "80cf95e3-9087-414b-acf0-376260ebe071", "type": "concept", "title": "Poetic language"}, {"id": "967b5d66-9908-438f-abd5-4d6409578955", "type": "concept", "title": "Latin American cultural autonomy"}, {"id": "4b79442a-8770-4475-bd96-1f0dd19e4b12", "type": "concept", "title": "Gender and decadence"}, {"id": "58e38d0c-e161-44fe-82c2-1d6e3c20fc37", "type": "concept", "title": "Transition to vanguardia"}], "description": "Students trace how modernismo shaped later poetry, essay, and national-cultural debates."}]}, {"id": "35725bb7-86a1-44c0-a4e6-1197a31eb07c", "type": "topic", "title": "Vanguardias, Poetry, and Experimental Form", "description": "This unit treats Latin American literature as a laboratory of form, where poetry, manifestos, little magazines, and experimental prose reimagined language itself.", "children": [{"id": "45fad757-4648-4e2b-8341-833f617acf0a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Avant-garde movements", "children": [{"id": "656d9ced-4c98-494c-ad1b-bd9a4c719839", "type": "concept", "title": "Creacionismo"}, {"id": "9c10bccb-22b0-4180-98a9-ef8de493bae5", "type": "concept", "title": "Ultraismo"}, {"id": "0eb9d583-43c7-4462-9ff0-8e680968ed9d", "type": "concept", "title": "Estridentismo"}, {"id": "9c59bcd9-95c6-465f-85a6-e22b4e7d8a0d", "type": "concept", "title": "Surrealist and local experiments"}], "description": "Students situate Latin American vanguardias in transatlantic and local contexts."}, {"id": "9d53efd9-1efa-4bff-8f7e-f1334fd6a9f6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Poetry and politics", "children": [{"id": "74ab1b98-e200-426b-97b4-a7c06a12f487", "type": "concept", "title": "Neruda and Vallejo"}, {"id": "e870b467-9587-4d1d-8495-45173d3d6858", "type": "concept", "title": "Public voice and lyric voice"}, {"id": "605b0bc4-972d-49c3-82e9-13172053001e", "type": "concept", "title": "Militancy and ambiguity"}, {"id": "c7fee4f9-5676-47aa-8b66-9ebabd2715da", "type": "concept", "title": "Poetry as collective address"}], "description": "Students examine how poetic innovation intersects with revolution, anti-imperialism, and social crisis."}, {"id": "d690d1e1-6e6f-45be-8998-c6b68e731950", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Experimental prose", "children": [{"id": "8ac04ecb-976a-4715-8b65-6ae5dcbda1c2", "type": "concept", "title": "Manifestos"}, {"id": "00d732b1-94aa-4b93-87fc-bf514532463f", "type": "concept", "title": "Urban montage"}, {"id": "8db2b1df-2040-4365-8946-3a789a764cf9", "type": "concept", "title": "Metafictional gestures"}, {"id": "1a81f0db-bcb0-45da-9262-d25d9c39d5c5", "type": "concept", "title": "Typography and visual form"}], "description": "Students read fragmented, urban, and self-conscious forms as responses to modernity."}]}, {"id": "d9809741-a0a3-4a04-8338-b4dfa260cb68", "type": "topic", "title": "Regionalism, Indigenismo, Afro-Latin American and Caribbean Writing", "description": "Students study how literature represents land, labor, race, language, and cultural mixture beyond capital-city literary centers.", "children": [{"id": "7db48a3c-557d-48d2-869e-59f160be4808", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Regionalism and the land", "children": [{"id": "3b4fb99f-966e-4de9-8072-bb5064a25dfe", "type": "concept", "title": "Novela de la tierra"}, {"id": "ef648199-5c0d-4511-8da0-aee7477651b5", "type": "concept", "title": "Frontier and jungle"}, {"id": "db843fdd-60dd-4c77-a604-2cbcc9627983", "type": "concept", "title": "Plantation and hacienda"}, {"id": "3cf92422-d401-407b-9ca9-926113f58e94", "type": "concept", "title": "Environment and extraction"}], "description": "Students analyze how landscape becomes a force in plots about labor, violence, and social hierarchy."}, {"id": "e12a7455-e287-40e3-bb52-855c699a33d7", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Indigenismo and its critiques", "children": [{"id": "d92ae235-6867-4986-86fc-6a9d828b3d22", "type": "concept", "title": "Jose Maria Arguedas"}, {"id": "3ef07de9-dd5a-4026-aef5-dc249e109bbf", "type": "concept", "title": "Mestizo mediation"}, {"id": "050c333d-f3d6-4865-a1b5-2004a87b09ee", "type": "concept", "title": "Language politics"}, {"id": "05a73d46-ac7d-4d10-9731-6f56ba583149", "type": "concept", "title": "From indigenismo to Indigenous authorship"}], "description": "Students examine advocacy, representation, and the problem of speaking for Indigenous communities."}, {"id": "a97c2125-0041-4fec-b45d-8fc44e32a432", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Afro-Latin American and Caribbean voices", "children": [{"id": "534d3d88-1a79-47dc-8e60-959de47354b7", "type": "concept", "title": "Negrismo and its limits"}, {"id": "82c53114-18e9-4ab0-9ba5-df93ee1673f0", "type": "concept", "title": "Afro-Caribbean poetry"}, {"id": "c50ad01f-855b-40ff-946e-281304f2be46", "type": "concept", "title": "Diaspora and creolization"}, {"id": "c39b1364-dfc6-41b4-b971-da9ca44f2e5c", "type": "concept", "title": "Race, music, and performance"}], "description": "Students foreground Black Atlantic, Caribbean, and Afro-descendant literary traditions often underrepresented in basic surveys."}]}, {"id": "63431fd3-db79-4386-a4db-ea58380f4dc6", "type": "topic", "title": "The Latin American Boom and Magical Realism", "description": "This unit studies the international visibility of the Boom while resisting the idea that Latin American literature can be reduced to magical realism.", "children": [{"id": "ff358526-dcf6-4661-87e3-4e7843471725", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Boom narrative innovation", "children": [{"id": "7a57cc6c-1cc4-4f07-852c-9529eccdd800", "type": "concept", "title": "Nonlinear narration"}, {"id": "efa47526-fa08-489c-b1a4-64391a4a2f84", "type": "concept", "title": "Multiple perspectives"}, {"id": "3e5accef-116d-4033-abbf-38808913b03f", "type": "concept", "title": "Metafiction"}, {"id": "568baeed-978f-40c9-a8de-ca3ca542d6e0", "type": "concept", "title": "Urban and political complexity"}], "description": "Students examine formal experimentation in novels that changed global perceptions of Latin American fiction."}, {"id": "72f1f0b4-017c-44b4-ad59-6ec133aa73ae", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Magical realism and its contexts", "children": [{"id": "ce4e1700-07e4-4301-b7e8-b00e477b42c3", "type": "concept", "title": "Everyday marvelous"}, {"id": "02d2bb76-3c6c-4eae-9235-0d15373daec0", "type": "concept", "title": "Community and myth"}, {"id": "b54dda64-5ce6-458f-a9bf-c695f4076958", "type": "concept", "title": "History and violence"}, {"id": "ca5a3a71-f905-4a59-96c6-8880c70554ce", "type": "concept", "title": "Reception and commodification"}], "description": "Students distinguish magical realism from fantasy, folklore, and market stereotypes."}, {"id": "3e32ee5d-bd81-4c26-bb81-fe3ed20669c9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Canon formation", "children": [{"id": "039b83a9-8130-4822-9800-b426ea1751a7", "type": "concept", "title": "Garcia Marquez, Cortazar, Fuentes, Vargas Llosa"}, {"id": "1a7f2ccf-21ff-446a-bc8c-3ee064a61272", "type": "concept", "title": "Publishing in Barcelona and beyond"}, {"id": "75d8a000-9e08-4d05-9e7d-f58ae4e9cadc", "type": "concept", "title": "Women writers and exclusions"}, {"id": "f695f848-1e5d-4cd6-992d-2e6fb8199cff", "type": "concept", "title": "World literature circuits"}], "description": "Students ask who became visible, who was left out, and how publishing markets shaped the Boom."}]}, {"id": "369cb80c-1499-43ef-92c4-5dc38b54b0bc", "type": "topic", "title": "Dictatorship, Testimonio, Memory, and Human Rights", "description": "Students study literature's role in witnessing state violence, preserving memory, and debating truth, representation, and justice.", "children": [{"id": "13648bf0-ed98-4bb6-b7d4-1fc97e16403e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Dictatorship and repression", "children": [{"id": "5aa3bc3c-28b1-4697-b851-aac68c66cab4", "type": "concept", "title": "Southern Cone dictatorships"}, {"id": "f1a721a4-a1f4-4abf-9ae3-05ba5822b989", "type": "concept", "title": "Central American conflicts"}, {"id": "89d618ca-6e46-4504-9c4f-b271e0a47bb5", "type": "concept", "title": "Exile writing"}, {"id": "1b4657e5-f2f1-488e-8f9a-c86fd7db3649", "type": "concept", "title": "Allegory and coded critique"}], "description": "Students connect literary form to censorship, exile, fear, and authoritarian power."}, {"id": "93a1ceae-c079-4ed1-9ba9-bf0252da1528", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Testimonio and witness", "children": [{"id": "54bbf9dd-41d0-4392-8468-d7f0042c0344", "type": "concept", "title": "I, we, and community voice"}, {"id": "de73dfff-076d-4afc-a80a-7e080759ebdb", "type": "concept", "title": "Editor and narrator"}, {"id": "6d3a1ad5-cd4e-4882-b83c-4584ee8db79b", "type": "concept", "title": "Authenticity debates"}, {"id": "a04ec273-2830-4924-aaa6-6ebd665f0994", "type": "concept", "title": "Ethics of reading suffering"}], "description": "Students analyze first-person narrative, collective voice, mediation, and the politics of truth."}, {"id": "fa4c5144-0d45-452f-9b55-688414ae6e24", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Memory and post-dictatorship", "children": [{"id": "e5b7927a-84ee-442d-a6f2-49dd87e9b9f4", "type": "concept", "title": "Memory work"}, {"id": "9eb3b633-be12-4578-b55b-da69b85ef3a9", "type": "concept", "title": "Disappearance and mourning"}, {"id": "e62fba94-aa28-4f0a-baf7-3f985059b57a", "type": "concept", "title": "Children of dictatorship"}, {"id": "cb94c399-a894-414e-9796-c74af24bd791", "type": "concept", "title": "Archives, trials, and silence"}], "description": "Students examine how fiction, poetry, and essay confront trauma after formal political transitions."}]}, {"id": "52edfb23-88ac-46d7-83dd-0d9952e202a4", "type": "topic", "title": "Gender, Sexuality, Race, and Migration", "description": "This unit foregrounds identity not as a side topic but as a set of literary problems: voice, body, family, nation, diaspora, and power.", "children": [{"id": "83402eea-9a91-4a01-8142-b08b3dc68307", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Feminist and gendered writing", "children": [{"id": "4b495aeb-e23c-46ba-b6f4-bd7dcff514d5", "type": "concept", "title": "Women writers and canon revision"}, {"id": "63fc1f70-2143-4441-964b-36499e050a90", "type": "concept", "title": "Domestic space"}, {"id": "80189457-d273-451f-9034-4a96d826256d", "type": "concept", "title": "Body and desire"}, {"id": "119148db-adf7-4137-b50e-81a1560c4225", "type": "concept", "title": "Feminist testimony and essay"}], "description": "Students examine how writers challenge domestic ideology, literary canons, and gendered violence."}, {"id": "e32bb083-823a-43cf-b1fb-4905562c6d26", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Queer and dissident voices", "children": [{"id": "472d7bf0-018b-41b6-8510-01feb28fb933", "type": "concept", "title": "Queer urban writing"}, {"id": "ed744dc2-7712-479e-9f85-7441c2839027", "type": "concept", "title": "Performance and camp"}, {"id": "921ef776-dae9-43c1-9219-1279b4ae4a81", "type": "concept", "title": "Censorship and visibility"}, {"id": "d59aedd2-b3f0-4d3b-ab18-5c7d2090ca37", "type": "concept", "title": "Chosen families and exile"}], "description": "Students read sexuality and gender nonconformity as formal, political, and cultural interventions."}, {"id": "99d6b424-68cc-43a3-8cc6-6e62ddf2da0c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Migration and diaspora", "children": [{"id": "7458925e-43d4-426d-a355-212fcd3938c8", "type": "concept", "title": "Exile and return"}, {"id": "e29f5f38-2f47-47f8-82bd-8a5507080f07", "type": "concept", "title": "Borderlands"}, {"id": "fdf4516b-8b00-4fa0-ab56-9647409fa47d", "type": "concept", "title": "Language mixing"}, {"id": "78205af7-0bdb-4b01-9fc9-eb3e4367df0d", "type": "concept", "title": "Memory and belonging"}], "description": "Students explore movement across borders, languages, and generations."}]}, {"id": "cc2bb4a8-0655-47a0-b221-e8cf4cf9f1af", "type": "topic", "title": "Contemporary Fiction, Urban Life, and Globalization", "description": "Students turn to late twentieth- and twenty-first-century literature shaped by neoliberalism, urbanization, media culture, crime, ecology, and global markets.", "children": [{"id": "776730cc-aedf-427e-b6b6-df3f3a47c1bd", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Post-Boom and McOndo debates", "children": [{"id": "e1d37a0d-11c8-4081-99f6-7429866bfba3", "type": "concept", "title": "Post-Boom fiction"}, {"id": "b2331d7a-a872-4c67-b438-1e2eae5ca4f9", "type": "concept", "title": "McOndo"}, {"id": "6b563636-9b8e-45fa-9e88-5b17367d62c7", "type": "concept", "title": "Dirty realism"}, {"id": "ad7a4bd8-a735-4b90-b8c5-3ad5386dc84b", "type": "concept", "title": "Pop culture and media"}], "description": "Students examine challenges to magical-realism expectations and new forms of urban/global writing."}, {"id": "bf5d9dc8-c92c-4a5d-bf0c-cee0671fd556", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Violence, crime, and precarity", "children": [{"id": "d8b2b46e-cdf0-4038-8d81-f05d1e21f61d", "type": "concept", "title": "Narco-narratives"}, {"id": "1b78f928-cf61-4709-b931-d0984e7886c7", "type": "concept", "title": "Noir and crime fiction"}, {"id": "eab0f7de-b4e1-4f23-944a-4b0e14b1de5c", "type": "concept", "title": "Gendered violence"}, {"id": "08a8d49a-08de-4afe-ba1d-362926fe2632", "type": "concept", "title": "Ethics of representing violence"}], "description": "Students analyze how contemporary texts represent drug wars, femicide, inequality, and everyday insecurity."}, {"id": "53d1c23d-71b8-42e2-83e4-1f8d5d6aceb2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ecology and global futures", "children": [{"id": "6db800f6-b677-4a2d-a4dc-ef589a1f902d", "type": "concept", "title": "Ecofiction"}, {"id": "6c7f5168-d31f-4443-aa9c-bde487e693fc", "type": "concept", "title": "Amazonia and extraction"}, {"id": "f1fe4753-c075-4196-bd3f-d32b21bd3774", "type": "concept", "title": "Speculative fiction"}, {"id": "4593a9ce-0316-4ee3-b78e-c960b714d43b", "type": "concept", "title": "Global South frameworks"}], "description": "Students connect literature to extractivism, climate crisis, Indigenous activism, and speculative futures."}]}, {"id": "1183fa9a-f8d3-4298-a36a-2e37096a575f", "type": "topic", "title": "U.S. Latino/a/x and Transnational Latin American Literature", "description": "A Latin American literature survey should include migration, diaspora, bilingualism, and U.S. Latino/a/x writing as part of a transnational literary field.", "children": [{"id": "e6cfa3c4-3179-4e03-8b5c-a0d3b3649851", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Latino/a/x literary formations", "children": [{"id": "e57640f3-f937-4250-8a59-4a1bdce8da9b", "type": "concept", "title": "Chicano/a literature"}, {"id": "e62ecf2b-0f11-46ea-be9d-007aa552d840", "type": "concept", "title": "Puerto Rican/Nuyorican writing"}, {"id": "7d59f7ed-909b-4495-bce8-02f1eda355d2", "type": "concept", "title": "Dominican-American writing"}, {"id": "7dd202b9-9963-46cc-b10f-4523a73b816b", "type": "concept", "title": "Central American diasporas"}], "description": "Students study how U.S.-based writers negotiate heritage, language, race, and national belongi